<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Ticker.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- No product dependency pulls FSharp.Core in as copy-local for a
         package-free console app; without this the built binary can't find
         it at run time even though it compiles fine. -->
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The counter below is held in a REGISTERED holder, which is what makes
         the app's live state visible to a hot-reload restart.

         This is a plain assembly Reference, NOT a ProjectReference, and both
         choices are forced by MEASURED failures, recorded here so neither is
         "simplified" back:

           1. A ProjectReference fails with NU1605 — it pulls FSharp.Core
              11.0.101-preview7.26381.103 transitively through
              FSharp.Compiler.Service, which the sample's own pinned
              FSharp.Core (…26359.118) reads as a downgrade, and the repo
              treats warnings as errors.

           2. A HintPath into SageFs.Core/bin is WRONG TOO, and the local gate
              caught it: it assumes SageFs.Core has already been built into
              that exact Configuration/TFM. In a clean checkout on a machine
              that has, the sample fails with FS0039 `HolderRegistry is not
              defined`. Building an assembly a sample points at is a build-
              order dependency on a path that may not exist yet.

         A user should write NEITHER. SageFs injects the reference at build
         time with `-p:CustomAfterMicrosoftCommonTargets=<generated>.targets`,
         which resolves the tool's own assembly at run time rather than
         guessing a path now. Verified to build and run this exact project with
         this Reference deleted. `-p:ReferencePath=` does NOT work for this: it
         only tells MSBuild where to look for references that already exist, so
         it can never add one.

         Until that injection lands, this Reference is here so the solution
         still builds, and it is the LAST thing to remove. -->
    <Reference Include="SageFs.Core" Condition="'$(SageFsCoreAssembly)' != ''">
      <HintPath>$(SageFsCoreAssembly)</HintPath>
      <Private>true</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
